# SwiftFormat configuration for district-ios.
#
# Deliberately minimal: the default rule set, with only the options that pin
# house style so `swiftformat --lint` in CI is reproducible. Opt-in rules are
# added alongside the code that justifies them.
#
# ⚠️ RUN `swiftformat .` FROM THE PROJECT ROOT (the directory holding this file).
# The tool discovers this file by walking up from each input path, so running it
# from anywhere else may not find this config for paths passed absolutely.
#
# CI runs `swiftformat --lint .` which formats nothing and exits non-zero on any
# file that would change.

# Swift version drives which rules are safe to apply (e.g. `any` on existentials,
# consistent `if let` shorthand).
# ⚠️ MUST TRACK the swift image in ci/Dockerfile and the workflow. A version
# stated here that is newer than the toolchain makes the formatter emit code the
# compiler rejects; older, and it silently skips modernisation rules.
--swiftversion 6.2

--indent 4
--maxwidth 120
--wraparguments before-first
--wrapparameters before-first
--wrapcollections before-first
--closingparen balanced
# ⚠️ `collections-only`, NOT the `always` default. `always` also adds a trailing
# comma to FUNCTION ARGUMENT LISTS, which is Swift 6.1+ syntax — so the
# formatter would emit code that only the newest parser accepts, and the first
# place that bites is the macOS tier's Xcode, not this Linux job. Collections
# have taken a trailing comma since Swift 1.
--trailing-commas collections-only
--trimwhitespace always
--stripunusedargs closure-only
--self remove
--importgrouping alpha

# Generated and vendored trees. `.build` is SwiftPM's output; DerivedData is
# Xcode's; SourcePackages holds the app's remote package checkouts (the workflow
# passes `-clonedSourcePackagesDirPath SourcePackages`). All are gitignored, but
# --lint would still walk them and take minutes.
--exclude .build,**/.build,DerivedData,**/DerivedData,SourcePackages,*.xcodeproj

# ⚠️ `redundantInit` OFF. It rewrites `Foo.init(...)` to `Foo(...)`, which is
# correct everywhere except when the initialiser is being referenced rather than
# called (`map(Foo.init)`), and the rule has historically got that wrong inside
# result-builder bodies. Cheap to re-enable later with a real diff to look at.
--disable redundantInit
